    a: "Chaos engineering deliberately injects controlled failure (killing an instance, adding network latency, exhausting a resource) into a system - ideally starting in staging, later in production with careful blast-radius limits - to verify your assumed resilience mechanisms (retries, failover, circuit breakers) actually work BEFORE a real, uncontrolled failure proves they don't. The key difference from accidental breakage is control: a defined hypothesis ('the system should survive this AZ going down'), a limited scope, and an immediate abort mechanism if the experiment goes worse than expected.",
