    q: "Design a disaster-recovery strategy for a stateful application (Postgres RDS + app tier) across an entire AWS region outage, and state your RTO/RPO tradeoffs.",
    sol: "# Warm standby design:\n# - RDS: a cross-region read replica in a second region gives near-zero RPO (replication lag,\n#   typically seconds) vs. restoring from a snapshot (RPO = time since last snapshot, potentially\n#   hours).\n# - App tier: keep the same Terraform config parametrized by region, ready to apply on demand\n#   (pilot light) if RTO tolerance allows the ~10-20 minutes it takes to stand up from scratch.\n# - DNS: Route 53 health-check-based failover routing pointing at the primary region, automatically\n#   failing over to the DR region's ALB if health checks fail for a sustained period.\n# - Runbook: promote the cross-region read replica to a standalone writable primary (this is a\n#   one-way, irreversible operation -- must be a deliberate, tested runbook step, not automatic,\n#   to avoid an accidental split-brain during a transient network partition that isn't a real\n#   regional outage).\n# RTO/RPO stated explicitly: replica promotion + DNS failover ~5-15 min RTO, RPO ~seconds --\n# cheaper than active-active (near-zero RTO/RPO but double cost and write-conflict complexity),\n# more expensive than backup-restore-only (near-zero cost, but RTO/RPO measured in hours).",
    exp: "The core tradeoff to articulate in an interview: DR cost scales roughly inversely with RTO/RPO -- backup-restore is cheap but slow with more data loss; active-active is fast with minimal loss but expensive and operationally complex; warm standby with a cross-region replica plus DNS failover is the common middle ground most teams actually run.",
