    q: "Design a zero-downtime database schema migration strategy for a rolling Kubernetes deployment where old and new Pod versions run simultaneously during the rollout.",
    sol: "# The core constraint: during a rolling update, OLD and NEW app code run against the SAME\n# database schema simultaneously for the rollout's duration.\n\n# Expand/contract pattern across (at least) 3 separate deploys:\n# 1. EXPAND: add the new column/table as NULLABLE (or with a default), deploy this schema\n#    change alone -- old app code ignores the new column entirely, still works unmodified.\n# 2. DUAL-WRITE / MIGRATE: deploy new app code that writes to BOTH old and new columns (or\n#    reads from new-with-fallback-to-old); backfill existing rows in a background job.\n# 3. CONTRACT: once ALL Pods run the new code and backfill is confirmed complete, deploy a\n#    final schema migration that drops the old column/constraint -- now safe since no running\n#    code references it anymore.",
    exp: "The interview red flag to avoid: a single migration that renames/drops a column and assumes the new app version is the only one running -- during a real rolling update, that instantly breaks every still-running old Pod for the entire rollout window, which is exactly the failure mode expand/contract is designed to prevent.",
