    q: "Write a CI script using terraform plan that detects configuration drift and fails the job if any is found, without making changes.",
    sol: '#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nterraform init -input=false\nset +e\nterraform plan -detailed-exitcode -out=plan.tfplan\nEXIT_CODE=$?\nset -e\n# 0 = no changes, 1 = error, 2 = changes present (drift or a real intended change)\nif [ "$EXIT_CODE" -eq 2 ]; then\n  echo "Drift or pending changes detected!" >&2\n  terraform show plan.tfplan\n  exit 1\nfi',
    exp: "-detailed-exitcode is what makes this scriptable -- plain terraform plan always exits 0 on success regardless of whether changes exist, so a CI job checking for drift specifically needs this flag to distinguish 'no changes' (0) from 'changes exist' (2).",
