    q: "Debug: an Ansible playbook run reports 'changed' on every single run for a task using the shell module to write a static config file, even though nothing actually changes. What's wrong, and how do you fix it?",
    sol: "# Problem: the shell/command modules are NOT idempotent by design -- Ansible has no way\n# to know if running the command again would produce a different result, so it always\n# reports 'changed' (unless you manually add changed_when: false, which just lies about it).\n\n# Fix: use a purpose-built, genuinely idempotent module instead:\n- name: Set app config\n  copy:\n    content: \"config=value\\n\"\n    dest: /etc/myapp/config.conf\n    mode: '0644'\n# or, for templated content:\n- name: Set app config\n  template:\n    src: config.conf.j2\n    dest: /etc/myapp/config.conf",
    exp: "shell/command are Ansible's escape hatch for arbitrary commands and are explicitly documented as non-idempotent -- copy/template/file/etc. actually check the current state (file content hash, permissions) before deciding whether a change is needed, which is what real idempotency requires.",
