    sol: "# The core tension: your NORMAL access path is IAM/SSO-gated (least privilege, MFA, audit\n# logging) -- but that path is exactly what might be unavailable during certain outages.\n\n# Break-glass design:\n# 1. A small number of PHYSICALLY separate emergency credentials, stored in a sealed process\n#    (a physical safe, or a Shamir's-Secret-Sharing-split secret requiring 2+ people to\n#    reconstruct -- no single person can use it alone).\n# 2. Using break-glass access is ALWAYS logged/alerted immediately to security/leadership the\n#    moment it's invoked -- not discovered after the fact in a routine audit.\n# 3. A mandatory post-incident review after every break-glass use, regardless of whether the\n#    outage was genuinely severe.\n# 4. Regularly TEST the break-glass procedure itself (e.g. quarterly) -- an emergency procedure\n#    nobody has ever actually exercised is likely to fail exactly when you need it most.",
    exp: "The interview-relevant tension to name explicitly: break-glass access is a deliberate, audited, rarely-used exception to normal least-privilege controls -- its own design must balance being genuinely usable during a real crisis against not becoming a permanent, unaudited backdoor that undermines the access model it's meant to be a fallback for.",
